/* Generated by sync-participant-notes.mjs from the two participant review files. */
(()=>{
const notes={"complete":{"1":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUse artificial intelligence (AI) deliberately and judge whether its work is useful.\n\n### Notes\n\nLab 1 produces a web page for your business. You supply the business facts, choose the working setup and review the result.\n\nThe practical skill is recognising what needs attention: missing information, the wrong tool, excessive access or a result that does not meet your standards. You do not need to become a programmer to make those decisions.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Use artificial intelligence (AI) deliberately and judge whether its work is useful.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Lab 1 produces a web page for your business. You supply the business facts, choose the working setup and review the result.</p><p>The practical skill is recognising what needs attention: missing information, the wrong tool, excessive access or a result that does not meet your standards. You do not need to become a programmer to make those decisions.</p>"},"2":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nSeparate the model from the software that lets it work with your information and tools.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**The model** processes the information it receives and generates a response.\n\n**The harness** is the surrounding software: it sends relevant information to the model, carries out permitted tool requests and returns the results. It is the bridge between the model and your files, applications and controls.\n\n### Notes\n\nAnthropic calls the combined Chat and Cowork interface the **new Claude experience**. It uses one conversation, without separate Chat and Cowork mode buttons. Rollout is gradual, so some accounts still show those buttons. Older screenshots may therefore show choices missing from your app.\n\nSeparate three questions: where are your files, where do tools run, and where does the model process information? These locations can differ.\n\nCurrent Cowork sessions run remotely. Access to your local files or computer features needs Claude Desktop open and connected, with the relevant access granted. The Code tab remains available separately; its local and cloud execution options appear on slide 17.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official explanation of Chat and Cowork becoming one Claude experience](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/16761823-claude-cowork-and-chat-are-one-claude)\n- [Official guide to Cowork’s capabilities and file access](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/13345190-get-started-with-claude-cowork)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Separate the model from the software that lets it work with your information and tools.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>The model</strong> processes the information it receives and generates a response.</p><p><strong>The harness</strong> is the surrounding software: it sends relevant information to the model, carries out permitted tool requests and returns the results. It is the bridge between the model and your files, applications and controls.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Anthropic calls the combined Chat and Cowork interface the <strong>new Claude experience</strong>. It uses one conversation, without separate Chat and Cowork mode buttons. Rollout is gradual, so some accounts still show those buttons. Older screenshots may therefore show choices missing from your app.</p><p>Separate three questions: where are your files, where do tools run, and where does the model process information? These locations can differ.</p><p>Current Cowork sessions run remotely. Access to your local files or computer features needs Claude Desktop open and connected, with the relevant access granted. The Code tab remains available separately; its local and cloud execution options appear on slide 17.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/16761823-claude-cowork-and-chat-are-one-claude\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official explanation of Chat and Cowork becoming one Claude experience</a></li><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/13345190-get-started-with-claude-cowork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to Cowork’s capabilities and file access</a></li></ul>"},"3":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nRecognise the five components you can use to understand and guide an AI system.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**An AI agent** combines a model with instructions and the ability to use tools to work towards a goal. Its usefulness depends on the surrounding setup, not just the model's type.\n\n### Notes\n\nUse the five components to diagnose a disappointing result:\n\n- Model: is its capability suitable?\n- Context: did it receive the right information?\n- Tools: can it perform the required task?\n- Human judgement: have you specified and checked the outcome?\n- Permissions: is the required action allowed?\n\nA stronger model will not fix a missing file or unavailable tool. Identify the problem before changing the setup.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Anthropic’s guide to building effective agents](https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Recognise the five components you can use to understand and guide an AI system.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>An AI agent</strong> combines a model with instructions and the ability to use tools to work towards a goal. Its usefulness depends on the surrounding setup, not just the model&#39;s type.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Use the five components to diagnose a disappointing result:</p><ul><li>Model: is its capability suitable?</li><li>Context: did it receive the right information?</li><li>Tools: can it perform the required task?</li><li>Human judgement: have you specified and checked the outcome?</li><li>Permissions: is the required action allowed?</li></ul><p>A stronger model will not fix a missing file or unavailable tool. Identify the problem before changing the setup.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Anthropic’s guide to building effective agents</a></li></ul>"},"4":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nIdentify the practical agents you can adapt to your own business.\n\n### Notes\n\n- **Web Builder:** create and improve a business website.\n- **Project Management & Operations Assistant:** organise information, prepare replies and coordinate everyday work.\n- **Search engine optimisation (SEO) Marketer:** investigate customer searches and improve your business’s online visibility.\n- **Lead Builder:** find and organise possible customers for your review.\n\nEach role becomes useful through your company information, instructions and decisions. Choose a specific business outcome rather than expecting one agent to manage everything.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Identify the practical agents you can adapt to your own business.</p><h3>Notes</h3><ul><li><strong>Web Builder:</strong> create and improve a business website.</li><li><strong>Project Management &amp; Operations Assistant:</strong> organise information, prepare replies and coordinate everyday work.</li><li><strong>Search engine optimisation (SEO) Marketer:</strong> investigate customer searches and improve your business’s online visibility.</li><li><strong>Lead Builder:</strong> find and organise possible customers for your review.</li></ul><p>Each role becomes useful through your company information, instructions and decisions. Choose a specific business outcome rather than expecting one agent to manage everything.</p>"},"5":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nDistinguish building your project from making its results available or running it automatically.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Claude Code** is Anthropic’s coding assistant: it reads project files, edits them and runs commands to build or improve software. We use it through the Code tab in the Claude Desktop app.\n\n**Claude Cowork** handles broader work such as research, reports and organising files. You describe a result, and it works through the steps using authorised tools. These capabilities are now joining ordinary conversation in the unified Claude experience.\n\n**Deployment** makes finished software available in its intended working environment.\n\n**platform.claude.com** provides developer access to Claude models and services for applications, separately from the Claude app.\n\n**A scheduled task** saves instructions and starts work automatically at a chosen time, such as checking your inbox each weekday morning.\n\n**An artefact** displays a finished output you can view or interact with, such as this presentation or a web page.\n\n### Notes\n\nThese are different ways to build, share or repeat work. A cloud task can run with your laptop off if its files and connections are available remotely. A local task needs its computer running.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop)\n- [Official introduction to Claude Code’s capabilities](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview)\n- [Official guidance on the unified Claude conversation](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/16761823-claude-cowork-and-chat-are-one-claude)\n- [Official overview of managed agents on Claude Platform](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/managed-agents/overview)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Distinguish building your project from making its results available or running it automatically.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Claude Code</strong> is Anthropic’s coding assistant: it reads project files, edits them and runs commands to build or improve software. We use it through the Code tab in the Claude Desktop app.</p><p><strong>Claude Cowork</strong> handles broader work such as research, reports and organising files. You describe a result, and it works through the steps using authorised tools. These capabilities are now joining ordinary conversation in the unified Claude experience.</p><p><strong>Deployment</strong> makes finished software available in its intended working environment.</p><p><strong>platform.claude.com</strong> provides developer access to Claude models and services for applications, separately from the Claude app.</p><p><strong>A scheduled task</strong> saves instructions and starts work automatically at a chosen time, such as checking your inbox each weekday morning.</p><p><strong>An artefact</strong> displays a finished output you can view or interact with, such as this presentation or a web page.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>These are different ways to build, share or repeat work. A cloud task can run with your laptop off if its files and connections are available remotely. A local task needs its computer running.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official introduction to Claude Code’s capabilities</a></li><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/16761823-claude-cowork-and-chat-are-one-claude\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guidance on the unified Claude conversation</a></li><li><a href=\"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/managed-agents/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official overview of managed agents on Claude Platform</a></li></ul>"},"6":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUnderstand why practical attempts matter as much as explanations.\n\n### Notes\n\nUse your own business information in the exercise. After each attempt, identify one concrete problem and test a clearer instruction.\n\nFor example, replace “make the page better” with “put the booking button above the first image and keep our existing colours.” Compare the next result with that request. Keep instructions that work so you can adapt them later.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Understand why practical attempts matter as much as explanations.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Use your own business information in the exercise. After each attempt, identify one concrete problem and test a clearer instruction.</p><p>For example, replace “make the page better” with “put the booking button above the first image and keep our existing colours.” Compare the next result with that request. Keep instructions that work so you can adapt them later.</p>"},"7":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nLocate the model within the five-component course map.\n\n### Notes\n\nThis section examines how the model produces an answer. It helps explain why useful context changes the result and why fluent answers still need checking.\n\nWhen an answer disappoints you, ask whether the problem is a missing business fact, a vague request or a claim that needs evidence. These require different corrections.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Locate the model within the five-component course map.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>This section examines how the model produces an answer. It helps explain why useful context changes the result and why fluent answers still need checking.</p><p>When an answer disappoints you, ask whether the problem is a missing business fact, a vague request or a claim that needs evidence. These require different corrections.</p>"},"8":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUnderstand how different inputs become model-readable units and count towards usage.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Tokens** are units a model processes. Text and code may become word fragments, punctuation or symbols, each represented by a number. **Tokenisation** performs that conversion.\n\nModels supporting other media use suitable numerical representations: images can be divided into patches; audio into short sections; and video into selected frames and audio. These contribute to token usage, although the exact conversion varies by model and format. Not every model accepts every media type.\n\n**API** means **Application Programming Interface**: a connection through which software requests a model’s services.\n\n### Notes\n\nInput tokens represent what the model receives; output tokens represent what it produces. The slide’s token numbers are examples, not a universal numbering system.\n\nAPI charges commonly depend on input and output tokens, with rates varying by model and token type. Subscription allowances also depend on the model, effort, reused material and tools. There is no universal conversion between tokens and a subscription allowance.\n\nUseful context earns its place; repeatedly sending large irrelevant files consumes resources without improving the answer.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official explanation of Claude usage limits and allowances](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9797557-usage-limit-best-practices)\n- [Google’s explanation of text image audio and video tokens](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/tokens)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Understand how different inputs become model-readable units and count towards usage.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Tokens</strong> are units a model processes. Text and code may become word fragments, punctuation or symbols, each represented by a number. <strong>Tokenisation</strong> performs that conversion.</p><p>Models supporting other media use suitable numerical representations: images can be divided into patches; audio into short sections; and video into selected frames and audio. These contribute to token usage, although the exact conversion varies by model and format. Not every model accepts every media type.</p><p><strong>API</strong> means <strong>Application Programming Interface</strong>: a connection through which software requests a model’s services.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Input tokens represent what the model receives; output tokens represent what it produces. The slide’s token numbers are examples, not a universal numbering system.</p><p>API charges commonly depend on input and output tokens, with rates varying by model and token type. Subscription allowances also depend on the model, effort, reused material and tools. There is no universal conversion between tokens and a subscription allowance.</p><p>Useful context earns its place; repeatedly sending large irrelevant files consumes resources without improving the answer.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9797557-usage-limit-best-practices\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official explanation of Claude usage limits and allowances</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/tokens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Google’s explanation of text image audio and video tokens</a></li></ul>"},"9":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUnderstand how training improves a model before you use it.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Training** adjusts a model using data and feedback. Developers such as Anthropic and other research teams do this before releasing the models you use.\n\n**Parameters** are the model’s internal numerical settings, not a database of verified facts.\n\n**Loss** measures how far a prediction is from its training target. Training repeatedly predicts, measures the error and adjusts parameters.\n\n### Notes\n\nBuilding a leading model from scratch requires substantial computing resources and can cost millions of dollars. You use an already trained model in this course; you do not need to train one.\n\nTraining is not exclusive to large companies: smaller organisations can train or adapt models too. That is a different activity from the exercises here.\n\nCorrecting an ordinary chat normally changes the information guiding the next response, not the model’s parameters. Improving your prompts and company guidance therefore does not require retraining.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [How artificial intelligence and machine learning fit together](https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/artificial-intelligence)\n- [DeepSeek’s technical report with a training-cost example](https://arxiv.org/html/2412.19437v2)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Understand how training improves a model before you use it.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Training</strong> adjusts a model using data and feedback. Developers such as Anthropic and other research teams do this before releasing the models you use.</p><p><strong>Parameters</strong> are the model’s internal numerical settings, not a database of verified facts.</p><p><strong>Loss</strong> measures how far a prediction is from its training target. Training repeatedly predicts, measures the error and adjusts parameters.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Building a leading model from scratch requires substantial computing resources and can cost millions of dollars. You use an already trained model in this course; you do not need to train one.</p><p>Training is not exclusive to large companies: smaller organisations can train or adapt models too. That is a different activity from the exercises here.</p><p>Correcting an ordinary chat normally changes the information guiding the next response, not the model’s parameters. Improving your prompts and company guidance therefore does not require retraining.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/artificial-intelligence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">How artificial intelligence and machine learning fit together</a></li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.19437v2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DeepSeek’s technical report with a training-cost example</a></li></ul>"},"11":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUnderstand how a trained model produces an answer without normally retraining.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Inference** means using an already trained model to process new input and produce output.\n\n**Next-token prediction** estimates which pieces of text could follow the information already supplied. The selected piece is added to the response, and the process repeats.\n\n### Notes\n\nThe capital-city example illustrates inference with the learned parameters held fixed. The response is produced piece by piece.\n\nThe percentages compare possible next tokens; they are not factual confidence scores. A likely continuation can still be false. Supply relevant information before asking, and verify important claims afterwards.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Understand how a trained model produces an answer without normally retraining.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Inference</strong> means using an already trained model to process new input and produce output.</p><p><strong>Next-token prediction</strong> estimates which pieces of text could follow the information already supplied. The selected piece is added to the response, and the process repeats.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>The capital-city example illustrates inference with the learned parameters held fixed. The response is produced piece by piece.</p><p>The percentages compare possible next tokens; they are not factual confidence scores. A likely continuation can still be false. Supply relevant information before asking, and verify important claims afterwards.</p>"},"12":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUnderstand answer variation and how clearer preferences improve relevance.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Sampling** selects among plausible next tokens. This introduces a random component: the same prompt can produce different answers. Random variation is not the only reason an answer can be wrong.\n\n### Notes\n\n“What is Australia’s best city?” leaves the criteria unspecified. Adding a preference for mid-size cities, warm dry summers and mild winters makes Adelaide a stronger match to that request, not the best city for everyone.\n\nThe percentages illustrate how context can shift an answer; they are not measured model outputs.\n\nIf an answer is irrelevant, identify a missing preference or constraint. Clearer criteria improve relevance without guaranteeing accuracy.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Understand answer variation and how clearer preferences improve relevance.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Sampling</strong> selects among plausible next tokens. This introduces a random component: the same prompt can produce different answers. Random variation is not the only reason an answer can be wrong.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>“What is Australia’s best city?” leaves the criteria unspecified. Adding a preference for mid-size cities, warm dry summers and mild winters makes Adelaide a stronger match to that request, not the best city for everyone.</p><p>The percentages illustrate how context can shift an answer; they are not measured model outputs.</p><p>If an answer is irrelevant, identify a missing preference or constraint. Clearer criteria improve relevance without guaranteeing accuracy.</p>"},"14":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nRecognise the Claude app as the environment for the practical exercises.\n\n### Notes\n\nUse the Claude Desktop app for the workshop’s Code exercises. Check the selected environment, project folder, model, effort and permissions before starting.\n\nIf a screenshot differs from your app, identify the equivalent control using its purpose and the current documentation. Interface changes do not necessarily mean a feature is unavailable.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Recognise the Claude app as the environment for the practical exercises.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Use the Claude Desktop app for the workshop’s Code exercises. Check the selected environment, project folder, model, effort and permissions before starting.</p><p>If a screenshot differs from your app, identify the equivalent control using its purpose and the current documentation. Interface changes do not necessarily mean a feature is unavailable.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app</a></li></ul>"},"15":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nChoose how to work with Claude according to the outcome and access needed.\n\n### Notes\n\nYour app may no longer show separate Chat and Cowork options; see slide 6 for the interface change. Compare the work you need rather than search for a missing button:\n\n- For a headline or paragraph, discuss alternatives in the conversation.\n- For a report or organised files, describe the finished result and provide the needed information and access.\n- For website files and previews, use Code to make and inspect project changes.\n\nThese are examples, not exclusive capabilities. Choose according to the files, tools and review steps required. The unified conversation can handle both quick questions and larger tasks; it does not automatically receive unlimited access.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official explanation of the new Claude experience and its controls](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/16761823-claude-cowork-and-chat-are-one-claude)\n- [Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Choose how to work with Claude according to the outcome and access needed.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Your app may no longer show separate Chat and Cowork options; see slide 6 for the interface change. Compare the work you need rather than search for a missing button:</p><ul><li>For a headline or paragraph, discuss alternatives in the conversation.</li><li>For a report or organised files, describe the finished result and provide the needed information and access.</li><li>For website files and previews, use Code to make and inspect project changes.</li></ul><p>These are examples, not exclusive capabilities. Choose according to the files, tools and review steps required. The unified conversation can handle both quick questions and larger tasks; it does not automatically receive unlimited access.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/16761823-claude-cowork-and-chat-are-one-claude\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official explanation of the new Claude experience and its controls</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app</a></li></ul>"},"16":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nIdentify which computer runs your project tools and holds the working files.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Local execution** means the project's commands and file operations run on your computer.\n\n**Cloud execution** means they run on a remote computer. It has its own working files and installed software.\n\n### Notes\n\nLocal work makes existing folders convenient to use and keeps editable files on your device. Cloud work can suit tasks whose resources are already accessible remotely.\n\nSelecting local Code does not move the model onto your laptop or make the work offline. Check the environment before installing software or choosing files.\n\nRemote Control changes how you reach a session, not where it executes: the computer actually running it still needs to be on.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Identify which computer runs your project tools and holds the working files.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Local execution</strong> means the project&#39;s commands and file operations run on your computer.</p><p><strong>Cloud execution</strong> means they run on a remote computer. It has its own working files and installed software.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Local work makes existing folders convenient to use and keeps editable files on your device. Cloud work can suit tasks whose resources are already accessible remotely.</p><p>Selecting local Code does not move the model onto your laptop or make the work offline. Check the environment before installing software or choosing files.</p><p>Remote Control changes how you reach a session, not where it executes: the computer actually running it still needs to be on.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app</a></li></ul>"},"17":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nChoose permission settings that keep important changes visible for review.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Manual mode** asks for approval for actions that require it, helping you inspect proposed changes.\n\n**Auto mode** delegates permission decisions to Claude's checks. It does not guarantee every action is harmless.\n\n**Accept edits** permits file edits automatically. **Bypass permissions** skips Claude's normal permission checks and removes an important safeguard.\n\n### Notes\n\nStart Lab 1 in Manual mode. Before approving an action, check the affected file or service and what will change.\n\nAvailable modes differ between environments. A familiar-looking request can affect a different folder or account, so check its details. “Be careful” in a prompt does not replace access controls.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to Claude Code permission modes](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Choose permission settings that keep important changes visible for review.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Manual mode</strong> asks for approval for actions that require it, helping you inspect proposed changes.</p><p><strong>Auto mode</strong> delegates permission decisions to Claude&#39;s checks. It does not guarantee every action is harmless.</p><p><strong>Accept edits</strong> permits file edits automatically. <strong>Bypass permissions</strong> skips Claude&#39;s normal permission checks and removes an important safeguard.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Start Lab 1 in Manual mode. Before approving an action, check the affected file or service and what will change.</p><p>Available modes differ between environments. A familiar-looking request can affect a different folder or account, so check its details. “Be careful” in a prompt does not replace access controls.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to Claude Code permission modes</a></li></ul>"},"18":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nCreate a dedicated local folder and select it as your website project.\n\n### Notes\n\nCreate Projects, then a Web-Builder subfolder: in your C: drive on Windows or your Home folder on Mac. Select Web-Builder through Open folder in Claude.\n\nKeep active coding work outside OneDrive, Google Drive and similar synchronised locations. Many file changes can produce confusing sync notifications; keep a separate backup.\n\nEditable project files are easier to reuse or move between tools. Conversation history and settings may live elsewhere and need separate export.\n\nThe chosen folder is a working starting point, not a guarantee that every tool is restricted to it.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Create a dedicated local folder and select it as your website project.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Create Projects, then a Web-Builder subfolder: in your C: drive on Windows or your Home folder on Mac. Select Web-Builder through Open folder in Claude.</p><p>Keep active coding work outside OneDrive, Google Drive and similar synchronised locations. Many file changes can produce confusing sync notifications; keep a separate backup.</p><p>Editable project files are easier to reuse or move between tools. Conversation history and settings may live elsewhere and need separate export.</p><p>The chosen folder is a working starting point, not a guarantee that every tool is restricted to it.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to Claude Code in the Desktop app</a></li></ul>"},"19":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nChoose a suitable model and effort level for the task.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Model choice** selects which trained model handles your request. Models differ in capability, speed and usage cost.\n\n**Effort** controls how much work a supported model puts into reasoning. Higher effort can take longer and use more of your allowance, even with the same model.\n\n### Notes\n\nStart with Sonnet and Medium effort for the workshop. Before increasing either, check whether the request contains the facts and constraints needed.\n\nUse more capability when the task or evaluated result justifies it. A short rewrite and a complex project-wide change need not use identical resources.\n\nThe Usage Credits control shows your account allowance. The context indicator, covered later, measures something different.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official explanation of Claude usage limits and allowances](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9797557-usage-limit-best-practices)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Choose a suitable model and effort level for the task.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Model choice</strong> selects which trained model handles your request. Models differ in capability, speed and usage cost.</p><p><strong>Effort</strong> controls how much work a supported model puts into reasoning. Higher effort can take longer and use more of your allowance, even with the same model.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Start with Sonnet and Medium effort for the workshop. Before increasing either, check whether the request contains the facts and constraints needed.</p><p>Use more capability when the task or evaluated result justifies it. A short rewrite and a complex project-wide change need not use identical resources.</p><p>The Usage Credits control shows your account allowance. The context indicator, covered later, measures something different.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9797557-usage-limit-best-practices\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official explanation of Claude usage limits and allowances</a></li></ul>"},"20":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nSave recurring preferences so you do not have to explain them every session.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Memory** is saved guidance that can be made available in later work. It might record your preferred spelling, answer length or a useful project convention.\n\n**Scope** means where guidance applies: one project or a wider set of your work.\n\n### Notes\n\nSave repeated preferences, not every one-off request. Business-specific guidance belongs with that project; a preference intended across projects needs the appropriate user-level location.\n\nReview the wording and destination before saving. Memory is not a complete transcript, and saved guidance is not guaranteed to load in every session. Check what is available when the task depends on it.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to persistent instructions and memory](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Save recurring preferences so you do not have to explain them every session.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Memory</strong> is saved guidance that can be made available in later work. It might record your preferred spelling, answer length or a useful project convention.</p><p><strong>Scope</strong> means where guidance applies: one project or a wider set of your work.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Save repeated preferences, not every one-off request. Business-specific guidance belongs with that project; a preference intended across projects needs the appropriate user-level location.</p><p>Review the wording and destination before saving. Memory is not a complete transcript, and saved guidance is not guaranteed to load in every session. Check what is available when the task depends on it.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to persistent instructions and memory</a></li></ul>"},"21":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nCreate a reusable company description through a short guided interview.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**A company description file** records your business facts in one reusable place: what you offer, who you help, how you differ and how you want to communicate.\n\n**Markdown** is plain text with simple formatting marks, such as # for a heading and - for a list.\n\n### Notes\n\nProvide the business, target customers, their needs and any branding guidelines. The practice prompt asks no more than five focused questions, one at a time.\n\nUseful details include services, location, customer problems, differentiators, evidence and website goals. Missing facts should remain unknown rather than be invented.\n\nApprove the structured summary before Claude creates My_Company_description.md in the project folder. Reuse this file for the website and Lab 2 instead of writing competing business descriptions.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Create a reusable company description through a short guided interview.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>A company description file</strong> records your business facts in one reusable place: what you offer, who you help, how you differ and how you want to communicate.</p><p><strong>Markdown</strong> is plain text with simple formatting marks, such as # for a heading and - for a list.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Provide the business, target customers, their needs and any branding guidelines. The practice prompt asks no more than five focused questions, one at a time.</p><p>Useful details include services, location, customer problems, differentiators, evidence and website goals. Missing facts should remain unknown rather than be invented.</p><p>Approve the structured summary before Claude creates My_Company_description.md in the project folder. Reuse this file for the website and Lab 2 instead of writing competing business descriptions.</p>"},"22":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUse an available skill when a task benefits from a repeatable specialist method.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**A skill** is a reusable package of task instructions, sometimes with supporting files or scripts. It tells Claude how to approach a type of work; it is not another AI model.\n\n**A plugin** can distribute skills and related capabilities together.\n\n### Notes\n\nFind the official frontend-design entry and select Add. Invoke the skill using the name shown; Code’s plugin form is /frontend-design:frontend-design.\n\nIf it is unavailable, check that it is enabled in the current environment and project. Availability in another Claude surface does not prove it is loaded here. A fresh session may help diagnose loading, but is not always required.\n\nReview third-party skill instructions and scripts before using them: they can influence actions, not just writing style.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to creating and invoking Claude Code skills](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Use an available skill when a task benefits from a repeatable specialist method.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>A skill</strong> is a reusable package of task instructions, sometimes with supporting files or scripts. It tells Claude how to approach a type of work; it is not another AI model.</p><p><strong>A plugin</strong> can distribute skills and related capabilities together.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Find the official frontend-design entry and select Add. Invoke the skill using the name shown; Code’s plugin form is /frontend-design:frontend-design.</p><p>If it is unavailable, check that it is enabled in the current environment and project. Availability in another Claude surface does not prove it is loaded here. A fresh session may help diagnose loading, but is not always required.</p><p>Review third-party skill instructions and scripts before using them: they can influence actions, not just writing style.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to creating and invoking Claude Code skills</a></li></ul>"},"23":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nChoose enough relevant information without overwhelming the task.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Context** is the information made available to the model for a particular response. It can include your messages, selected file content, instructions and tool results.\n\n### Notes\n\nFor the website, start with the company description, branding, selected images and specific design references. Add other material only when it helps the task.\n\nCheck whether each source is relevant, current and consistent. Remove outdated instructions or identify which version should take precedence. A large file archive is not automatically useful context.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official explanation of Claude Code’s context and session lifecycle](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/how-claude-code-works)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Choose enough relevant information without overwhelming the task.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Context</strong> is the information made available to the model for a particular response. It can include your messages, selected file content, instructions and tool results.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>For the website, start with the company description, branding, selected images and specific design references. Add other material only when it helps the task.</p><p>Check whether each source is relevant, current and consistent. Remove outdated instructions or identify which version should take precedence. A large file archive is not automatically useful context.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/how-claude-code-works\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official explanation of Claude Code’s context and session lifecycle</a></li></ul>"},"24":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nInspect the information loaded into your session and distinguish capacity from quality.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**A context window** is the amount of information the model can work with in one request.\n\n**Compaction** shortens earlier conversation material into a summary so work can continue. Some detail may no longer be present in its original form.\n\n### Notes\n\nInspect the Context panel to see what is loaded and how much space remains. Capacity does not measure relevance: a full window can still contain the wrong material.\n\nSave confirmed decisions and unfinished work in a project file before ending a long session. Review the file and explicitly make it available next time.\n\nContext space and account usage allowance are separate limits. Saving a file does not guarantee that a future session will load it.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official explanation of Claude Code’s context and session lifecycle](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/how-claude-code-works)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Inspect the information loaded into your session and distinguish capacity from quality.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>A context window</strong> is the amount of information the model can work with in one request.</p><p><strong>Compaction</strong> shortens earlier conversation material into a summary so work can continue. Some detail may no longer be present in its original form.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Inspect the Context panel to see what is loaded and how much space remains. Capacity does not measure relevance: a full window can still contain the wrong material.</p><p>Save confirmed decisions and unfinished work in a project file before ending a long session. Review the file and explicitly make it available next time.</p><p>Context space and account usage allowance are separate limits. Saving a file does not guarantee that a future session will load it.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/how-claude-code-works\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official explanation of Claude Code’s context and session lifecycle</a></li></ul>"},"25":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUnderstand what changes when an AI system can access an external service.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**A tool** is a capability the surrounding software can carry out when requested, such as reading a file or searching a mailbox.\n\n**A connector** links an account or service so selected information and actions can become available to Claude.\n\n### Notes\n\nConnected services may expose information beyond the current project folder. Check the account, readable information, permitted changes and disconnection method before connecting.\n\nStart with the access required for one contained task. Being connected does not prove every action is available or approved; test the specific capability you need.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to connecting Claude Code to external tools](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Understand what changes when an AI system can access an external service.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>A tool</strong> is a capability the surrounding software can carry out when requested, such as reading a file or searching a mailbox.</p><p><strong>A connector</strong> links an account or service so selected information and actions can become available to Claude.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Connected services may expose information beyond the current project folder. Check the account, readable information, permitted changes and disconnection method before connecting.</p><p>Start with the access required for one contained task. Being connected does not prove every action is available or approved; test the specific capability you need.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to connecting Claude Code to external tools</a></li></ul>"},"26":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUnderstand how MCP connects Claude to tools and information from other systems.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**MCP**, short for Model Context Protocol, is a shared way for AI applications to discover and use tools and information supplied by connected systems. Think of it as an agreed connection format rather than a separate model.\n\n**An MCP server** provides those capabilities to the application using the model.\n\n### Notes\n\nA calendar search can return an event without granting permission to create or delete one. The connection format does not decide the access level.\n\nUse /mcp in Code, or Connectors in the app’s Settings, to inspect what is available. The same service can expose different actions in different environments. A website address is not a substitute for an authorised account connection.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to connecting Claude Code to external tools](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp)\n- [Introduction to Model Context Protocol connections](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/introduction)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Understand how MCP connects Claude to tools and information from other systems.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>MCP</strong>, short for Model Context Protocol, is a shared way for AI applications to discover and use tools and information supplied by connected systems. Think of it as an agreed connection format rather than a separate model.</p><p><strong>An MCP server</strong> provides those capabilities to the application using the model.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>A calendar search can return an event without granting permission to create or delete one. The connection format does not decide the access level.</p><p>Use /mcp in Code, or Connectors in the app’s Settings, to inspect what is available. The same service can expose different actions in different environments. A website address is not a substitute for an authorised account connection.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to connecting Claude Code to external tools</a></li><li><a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/introduction\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Introduction to Model Context Protocol connections</a></li></ul>"},"28":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUse prompts you understand and can adapt to your own work.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**A prompt** is the instruction or request you give the AI. It can state the task, relevant background, constraints and the result you want.\n\n### Notes\n\nBefore using a template, replace its placeholders and check its assumptions. Be able to explain what it asks Claude to read, create or change.\n\nIf the instruction is unclear, ask Claude to explain the planned actions before running them. Adapt the request to your business rather than depending on exactly the same wording each time.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Use prompts you understand and can adapt to your own work.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>A prompt</strong> is the instruction or request you give the AI. It can state the task, relevant background, constraints and the result you want.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Before using a template, replace its placeholders and check its assumptions. Be able to explain what it asks Claude to read, create or change.</p><p>If the instruction is unclear, ask Claude to explain the planned actions before running them. Adapt the request to your business rather than depending on exactly the same wording each time.</p>"},"29":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nJudge an AI result and give specific instructions for improving it.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Evaluation** means comparing the result with your intended outcome and standards.\n\n**Iteration** means using that evaluation to make a targeted change, then checking the new result.\n\n### Notes\n\nTurn a judgement into an observable correction: “The first image should look visibly blurry; keep the same subject.” This is more useful than “make it better.”\n\nCheck whether the revision addressed the issue and damaged anything that already worked. Stop when the result meets the intended purpose, not when you run out of possible changes.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Judge an AI result and give specific instructions for improving it.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Evaluation</strong> means comparing the result with your intended outcome and standards.</p><p><strong>Iteration</strong> means using that evaluation to make a targeted change, then checking the new result.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Turn a judgement into an observable correction: “The first image should look visibly blurry; keep the same subject.” This is more useful than “make it better.”</p><p>Check whether the revision addressed the issue and damaged anything that already worked. Stop when the result meets the intended purpose, not when you run out of possible changes.</p>"},"30":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nConfirm your setup before beginning a new working session.\n\n### Notes\n\nBefore starting, confirm:\n\n- the working mode and local or cloud environment;\n- the selected project folder;\n- a suitable model and effort level;\n- relevant, consistent context;\n- the intended approval settings.\n\nIf a file or tool is missing, check the setup before rewriting the prompt. A local file may be unavailable in a cloud session, and a connected service may not expose the requested action.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Confirm your setup before beginning a new working session.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Before starting, confirm:</p><ul><li>the working mode and local or cloud environment;</li><li>the selected project folder;</li><li>a suitable model and effort level;</li><li>relevant, consistent context;</li><li>the intended approval settings.</li></ul><p>If a file or tool is missing, check the setup before rewriting the prompt. A local file may be unavailable in a cloud session, and a connected service may not expose the requested action.</p>"},"31":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUse your prepared business information and assets to direct the creation of a web page.\n\n### Notes\n\nUse My_Company_description.md as the business source. Apply its brand colours when defined; otherwise identify a colour reference.\n\nGive each inspiration site a purpose: layout, typography, navigation or a specific section. Request an original design rather than copied branding or text.\n\nProvide images through project files, an image-folder path or web addresses, and check usage rights.\n\nReplace prompt placeholders with the target visitor, main offer and desired action. Use relevant memory, skills and authorised connections. Ask for phone and desktop layouts, not just one attractive screenshot.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Use your prepared business information and assets to direct the creation of a web page.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Use My_Company_description.md as the business source. Apply its brand colours when defined; otherwise identify a colour reference.</p><p>Give each inspiration site a purpose: layout, typography, navigation or a specific section. Request an original design rather than copied branding or text.</p><p>Provide images through project files, an image-folder path or web addresses, and check usage rights.</p><p>Replace prompt placeholders with the target visitor, main offer and desired action. Use relevant memory, skills and authorised connections. Ask for phone and desktop layouts, not just one attractive screenshot.</p>"},"32":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nTurn your website review into a prioritised set of improvements.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**A call to action** tells a visitor what to do next, such as book a consultation or request a quote.\n\n**Accessibility** means making the page usable by people with different needs, including people using keyboards or assistive technology.\n\n### Notes\n\nCheck factual claims, the offer, audience fit, colours, typography, spacing, navigation, calls to action, images and phone/desktop layouts.\n\nPrioritise what prevents a visitor understanding or using the page. Give specific corrections, such as: “Use this reference’s spacing, retain our colours and keep the booking button visible on mobile.”\n\nTest links, buttons and forms. A form’s appearance does not prove submissions are stored or delivered. Recheck the page after revisions, including features that previously worked.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Turn your website review into a prioritised set of improvements.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>A call to action</strong> tells a visitor what to do next, such as book a consultation or request a quote.</p><p><strong>Accessibility</strong> means making the page usable by people with different needs, including people using keyboards or assistive technology.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Check factual claims, the offer, audience fit, colours, typography, spacing, navigation, calls to action, images and phone/desktop layouts.</p><p>Prioritise what prevents a visitor understanding or using the page. Give specific corrections, such as: “Use this reference’s spacing, retain our colours and keep the booking button visible on mobile.”</p><p>Test links, buttons and forms. A form’s appearance does not prove submissions are stored or delivered. Recheck the page after revisions, including features that previously worked.</p>"},"35":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUse a short corrective instruction when a conversation loses focus.\n\n### Notes\n\nChoose the correction that fits the problem:\n\n- Stay concise: set a useful answer length.\n- Step back: restate the outcome.\n- Verify: request evidence for a doubtful claim.\n- Summarise first: check understanding before changes.\n- Change only: name the edit and protect the rest.\n- Cut repetition: remove duplicate content.\n- Explain why: ask for the basis of a recommendation.\n\nFor example: “Summarise the three changes I requested before editing. Wait for approval.” Adding every instruction to every request can create conflict; use the correction that solves the problem.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Use a short corrective instruction when a conversation loses focus.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Choose the correction that fits the problem:</p><ul><li>Stay concise: set a useful answer length.</li><li>Step back: restate the outcome.</li><li>Verify: request evidence for a doubtful claim.</li><li>Summarise first: check understanding before changes.</li><li>Change only: name the edit and protect the rest.</li><li>Cut repetition: remove duplicate content.</li><li>Explain why: ask for the basis of a recommendation.</li></ul><p>For example: “Summarise the three changes I requested before editing. Wait for approval.” Adding every instruction to every request can create conflict; use the correction that solves the problem.</p>"},"40":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUnderstand the relationship between AI, machine learning, deep learning and generative AI.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Artificial intelligence** is the broad field of computer systems performing tasks associated with intelligence, such as recognising speech or solving problems.\n\n**Machine learning** is an approach within AI that learns patterns from data instead of relying entirely on manually written rules.\n\n**Deep learning** uses neural networks with many layers to learn patterns. A neural network is a system of connected calculations whose internal values are adjusted during training.\n\n**Generative AI** creates content, such as text, images or code, using patterns learned during training.\n\n### Notes\n\nThe nesting is a simplified map of modern AI technologies, not a ranking from weak to strong. Modern generative tools commonly use deep learning, but the diagram is not a universal classification of every historical method.\n\nGenerating fluent content does not mean the system has checked its claims.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [How artificial intelligence and machine learning fit together](https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/artificial-intelligence)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Understand the relationship between AI, machine learning, deep learning and generative AI.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Artificial intelligence</strong> is the broad field of computer systems performing tasks associated with intelligence, such as recognising speech or solving problems.</p><p><strong>Machine learning</strong> is an approach within AI that learns patterns from data instead of relying entirely on manually written rules.</p><p><strong>Deep learning</strong> uses neural networks with many layers to learn patterns. A neural network is a system of connected calculations whose internal values are adjusted during training.</p><p><strong>Generative AI</strong> creates content, such as text, images or code, using patterns learned during training.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>The nesting is a simplified map of modern AI technologies, not a ranking from weak to strong. Modern generative tools commonly use deep learning, but the diagram is not a universal classification of every historical method.</p><p>Generating fluent content does not mean the system has checked its claims.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/artificial-intelligence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">How artificial intelligence and machine learning fit together</a></li></ul>"},"41":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nCompare recognising patterns, creating content and taking actions towards a goal.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Traditional AI**, in this comparison, covers systems used to classify, predict or follow rules. For example, a system might route a customer enquiry.\n\n**Supervised learning** uses examples with supplied answers. **Self-supervised learning** creates learning tasks from the data itself, such as predicting missing or following text.\n\n### Notes\n\nThese capabilities overlap. An agentic workflow might use generative AI to draft a reply, then a tool to save it.\n\n“Continuous learning” in the comparison does not mean an agent retrains its model after every task. It may simply use new information to choose its next step.\n\nAutonomy concerns the actions it can take. Human oversight should match the consequences of mistakes, not decrease automatically as autonomy grows.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Anthropic’s guide to building effective agents](https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Compare recognising patterns, creating content and taking actions towards a goal.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Traditional AI</strong>, in this comparison, covers systems used to classify, predict or follow rules. For example, a system might route a customer enquiry.</p><p><strong>Supervised learning</strong> uses examples with supplied answers. <strong>Self-supervised learning</strong> creates learning tasks from the data itself, such as predicting missing or following text.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>These capabilities overlap. An agentic workflow might use generative AI to draft a reply, then a tool to save it.</p><p>“Continuous learning” in the comparison does not mean an agent retrains its model after every task. It may simply use new information to choose its next step.</p><p>Autonomy concerns the actions it can take. Human oversight should match the consequences of mistakes, not decrease automatically as autonomy grows.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Anthropic’s guide to building effective agents</a></li></ul>"},"42":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nApply six responsible-AI practices to your own business.\n\n### Notes\n\nThe Australian government’s foundations guidance provides six checks:\n\n1. **Own responsibility:** name who is accountable and set rules for use.\n2. **Consider impacts:** identify affected people and how they can question problems.\n3. **Manage risks:** identify possible harms and suitable safeguards.\n4. **Share information:** record AI uses and disclose them where appropriate.\n5. **Test and monitor:** check supplier evidence, test before relying on outputs and monitor performance. Include data protection and cybersecurity.\n6. **Maintain control:** ensure someone can review, intervene or stop the work.\n\nScale these practices to the consequences. A draft social post and an automated customer decision need different safeguards. Supplier assurances do not replace your responsibility for how the business uses AI.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Australian government’s six practices for responsible AI use](https://www.ai.gov.au/staying-safe-and-responsible/essential-ai-practices/guidance-ai-adoption-foundations)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Apply six responsible-AI practices to your own business.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>The Australian government’s foundations guidance provides six checks:</p><ol><li><strong>Own responsibility:</strong> name who is accountable and set rules for use.</li><li><strong>Consider impacts:</strong> identify affected people and how they can question problems.</li><li><strong>Manage risks:</strong> identify possible harms and suitable safeguards.</li><li><strong>Share information:</strong> record AI uses and disclose them where appropriate.</li><li><strong>Test and monitor:</strong> check supplier evidence, test before relying on outputs and monitor performance. Include data protection and cybersecurity.</li><li><strong>Maintain control:</strong> ensure someone can review, intervene or stop the work.</li></ol><p>Scale these practices to the consequences. A draft social post and an automated customer decision need different safeguards. Supplier assurances do not replace your responsibility for how the business uses AI.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.ai.gov.au/staying-safe-and-responsible/essential-ai-practices/guidance-ai-adoption-foundations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Australian government’s six practices for responsible AI use</a></li></ul>"},"43":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nMake the finished website available through a shareable hosted version.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Hosting** places a website's files on a service that can deliver them to visitors online. Seeing a local preview on your computer does not make the page publicly available.\n\n### Notes\n\nAsk Claude to create an artefact from the finished website while preserving its content, images and design. Preview it, check sharing settings and test the link as an intended visitor. Access may depend on plan or organisation settings.\n\nNetlify is an alternative for sites made of **Hypertext Markup Language (HTML)** and supporting assets. The guided prompt prepares a netlify-site folder containing index.html and the required files, then walks through uploading it. Everything published must be suitable for public access.\n\nHosting does not automatically add payments, a database or form delivery. Test those services separately and retain the editable site files.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to creating artefacts from Claude Code](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/artifacts)\n- [Official guide to artefact sharing and viewer access](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9547008-share-artifacts)\n- [Netlify’s guide to publishing a website by upload](https://docs.netlify.com/start/quickstarts/netlify-drop-quickstart/)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Make the finished website available through a shareable hosted version.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Hosting</strong> places a website&#39;s files on a service that can deliver them to visitors online. Seeing a local preview on your computer does not make the page publicly available.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Ask Claude to create an artefact from the finished website while preserving its content, images and design. Preview it, check sharing settings and test the link as an intended visitor. Access may depend on plan or organisation settings.</p><p>Netlify is an alternative for sites made of <strong>Hypertext Markup Language (HTML)</strong> and supporting assets. The guided prompt prepares a netlify-site folder containing index.html and the required files, then walks through uploading it. Everything published must be suitable for public access.</p><p>Hosting does not automatically add payments, a database or form delivery. Test those services separately and retain the editable site files.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/artifacts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to creating artefacts from Claude Code</a></li><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9547008-share-artifacts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to artefact sharing and viewer access</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/start/quickstarts/netlify-drop-quickstart/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Netlify’s guide to publishing a website by upload</a></li></ul>"},"44":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nKnow who is involved in the course and how to stay connected.\n\n### Notes\n\nMLAI (Machine Learning and Artificial Intelligence) is a community-led organisation helping Australian founders build startups using AI.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Explore MLAI’s founder community and activities](https://mlai.au)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Know who is involved in the course and how to stay connected.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>MLAI (Machine Learning and Artificial Intelligence) is a community-led organisation helping Australian founders build startups using AI.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://mlai.au\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Explore MLAI’s founder community and activities</a></li></ul>"}},"lab2":{"101":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nBuild an artificial intelligence (AI) assistant for everyday business work.\n\n### Notes\n\nStart with your own email-writing examples. Turn the patterns you approve into reusable guidance, then use it for drafts and a recurring inbox briefing.\n\nLater exercises add meeting minutes, task briefings, calendar preparation and follow-ups. Choose the workflow that solves a real problem before adding more automation.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Build an artificial intelligence (AI) assistant for everyday business work.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Start with your own email-writing examples. Turn the patterns you approve into reusable guidance, then use it for drafts and a recurring inbox briefing.</p><p>Later exercises add meeting minutes, task briefings, calendar preparation and follow-ups. Choose the workflow that solves a real problem before adding more automation.</p>"},"102":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nReuse your Lab 1 setup and company information for a new project.\n\n### Notes\n\nCreate and select PM-ops-assistant as the project folder: PM means project management and ops means operations. Point Claude to My_Company_description.md in the existing Web-Builder folder; do not create a second business description.\n\nCheck the environment, model, effort and approval settings before connecting business accounts. Load only the information needed for the task.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Reuse your Lab 1 setup and company information for a new project.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Create and select PM-ops-assistant as the project folder: PM means project management and ops means operations. Point Claude to My_Company_description.md in the existing Web-Builder folder; do not create a second business description.</p><p>Check the environment, model, effort and approval settings before connecting business accounts. Load only the information needed for the task.</p>"},"103":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nChoose practical ways an assistant can reduce everyday coordination work.\n\n### Notes\n\nUse the assistant for a specific result:\n\n- prioritise email and prepare replies;\n- extract meeting decisions, owners and next steps;\n- organise approved tasks with deadlines and status;\n- prepare meeting background from your calendar and records.\n\nStart with something you can inspect, such as two drafts or one morning briefing. “Manage my business” leaves too many decisions undefined.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Choose practical ways an assistant can reduce everyday coordination work.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Use the assistant for a specific result:</p><ul><li>prioritise email and prepare replies;</li><li>extract meeting decisions, owners and next steps;</li><li>organise approved tasks with deadlines and status;</li><li>prepare meeting background from your calendar and records.</li></ul><p>Start with something you can inspect, such as two drafts or one morning briefing. “Manage my business” leaves too many decisions undefined.</p>"},"104":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nEstablish useful access and clear boundaries before asking Claude to work.\n\n### Notes\n\nFor each workflow, separate the information it needs from the actions it may take. Reading a calendar does not require permission to book meetings; analysing email does not require permission to send replies.\n\nConnect the relevant services, inspect their available actions and set permissions before testing. This prevents a useful read task from quietly receiving unnecessary writing or deletion access.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Establish useful access and clear boundaries before asking Claude to work.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>For each workflow, separate the information it needs from the actions it may take. Reading a calendar does not require permission to book meetings; analysing email does not require permission to send replies.</p><p>Connect the relevant services, inspect their available actions and set permissions before testing. This prevents a useful read task from quietly receiving unnecessary writing or deletion access.</p>"},"105":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nConnect the services your chosen workflow needs.\n\n### Notes\n\nOpen the account menu in Claude Desktop, then Settings > Connectors.\n\nGoogle users connect Gmail, Google Drive and Google Calendar. Microsoft users use Microsoft 365 for supported Outlook, calendar and file access. The Microsoft connector requires a work account and may need organisational approval.\n\nSign into the intended account and review its requested access. Confirm the working session can use the tools before starting. You do not need to build a custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) server for this exercise.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to Gmail Drive and Calendar connections](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/10166901-use-google-workspace-connectors)\n- [Official Microsoft 365 connection requirements and setup](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/15183774-connect-to-microsoft-365)\n- [Official guide to connecting Claude Code to external tools](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Connect the services your chosen workflow needs.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Open the account menu in Claude Desktop, then Settings &gt; Connectors.</p><p>Google users connect Gmail, Google Drive and Google Calendar. Microsoft users use Microsoft 365 for supported Outlook, calendar and file access. The Microsoft connector requires a work account and may need organisational approval.</p><p>Sign into the intended account and review its requested access. Confirm the working session can use the tools before starting. You do not need to build a custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) server for this exercise.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/10166901-use-google-workspace-connectors\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to Gmail Drive and Calendar connections</a></li><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/15183774-connect-to-microsoft-365\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official Microsoft 365 connection requirements and setup</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to connecting Claude Code to external tools</a></li></ul>"},"106":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nGive the assistant only the authority needed for the current task.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**Least privilege** means granting the smallest useful amount of access. For example, analysing sent messages needs reading access, not permission to send replies or delete email.\n\n### Notes\n\nInspect the connector’s tool permissions: Always allow, Needs approval or Blocked where available.\n\nBegin with the reads needed for analysis. Approve draft creation or sending your own briefing separately when an exercise needs it. Block unrelated deletion, moving and labelling.\n\nAn unattended task may pause if approval is required. Test its intended permissions rather than allowing every action to make the pause disappear. Prompt instructions cannot remove access already granted in the interface.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to Claude Code permission modes](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions)\n- [Security guidance for Model Context Protocol connections](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/basic/security_best_practices)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Give the assistant only the authority needed for the current task.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>Least privilege</strong> means granting the smallest useful amount of access. For example, analysing sent messages needs reading access, not permission to send replies or delete email.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Inspect the connector’s tool permissions: Always allow, Needs approval or Blocked where available.</p><p>Begin with the reads needed for analysis. Approve draft creation or sending your own briefing separately when an exercise needs it. Block unrelated deletion, moving and labelling.</p><p>An unattended task may pause if approval is required. Test its intended permissions rather than allowing every action to make the pause disappear. Prompt instructions cannot remove access already granted in the interface.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to Claude Code permission modes</a></li><li><a href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/basic/security_best_practices\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Security guidance for Model Context Protocol connections</a></li></ul>"},"107":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nConfirm the assistant can perform small, relevant reads before starting the exercise.\n\n### Notes\n\nRequest one sent-email subject and date, one recent file title and one upcoming calendar event. Require Passed, Failed or Unavailable with evidence for each result.\n\nKeep the test read-only. If it fails, check the account, environment and permissions before expanding access.\n\nA successful read proves that read worked. It does not prove draft creation or email delivery. Those need separate tests when the workflow reaches them; describing how a tool works is not proof of a successful action.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to connecting Claude Code to external tools](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Confirm the assistant can perform small, relevant reads before starting the exercise.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Request one sent-email subject and date, one recent file title and one upcoming calendar event. Require Passed, Failed or Unavailable with evidence for each result.</p><p>Keep the test read-only. If it fails, check the account, environment and permissions before expanding access.</p><p>A successful read proves that read worked. It does not prove draft creation or email delivery. Those need separate tests when the workflow reaches them; describing how a tool works is not proof of a successful action.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to connecting Claude Code to external tools</a></li></ul>"},"108":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nDecide what the assistant should do and what you must review.\n\n### Notes\n\nChoose suitable examples, confirm the writing guidance and decide whether the draft is appropriate for the relationship.\n\nWatch for promises your business cannot fulfil, assumptions about the recipient and language that does not sound like you. Ask for supporting evidence before approving consequential actions.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Decide what the assistant should do and what you must review.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Choose suitable examples, confirm the writing guidance and decide whether the draft is appropriate for the relationship.</p><p>Watch for promises your business cannot fulfil, assumptions about the recipient and language that does not sound like you. Ask for supporting evidence before approving consequential actions.</p>"},"109":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nSee how different prompt detail changes the usefulness of an email-style analysis.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**A communication profile** is a short set of writing guidelines based on your own messages: tone, formality, sentence length, greetings, vocabulary and sign-offs.\n\n### Notes\n\nThe three prompts analyse the same writing sample. Added detail supplies selection rules, evidence and an output format; it should not change the task.\n\nStart with 20 suitable sent emails and the existing company file. Exclude automated messages, quoted replies by other people and sensitive material. Expand to 50 only if useful.\n\nCheck repeated patterns against examples and mark weak evidence. If comparing prompts, keep the email sample and settings consistent so you can interpret the differences.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>See how different prompt detail changes the usefulness of an email-style analysis.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>A communication profile</strong> is a short set of writing guidelines based on your own messages: tone, formality, sentence length, greetings, vocabulary and sign-offs.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>The three prompts analyse the same writing sample. Added detail supplies selection rules, evidence and an output format; it should not change the task.</p><p>Start with 20 suitable sent emails and the existing company file. Exclude automated messages, quoted replies by other people and sensitive material. Expand to 50 only if useful.</p><p>Check repeated patterns against examples and mark weak evidence. If comparing prompts, keep the email sample and settings consistent so you can interpret the differences.</p>"},"110":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nUnderstand a file-writing request before approving it.\n\n### Notes\n\nUse the approved analysis to create Communication_Profile.md. Save reusable guidance, short anonymised examples, evidence references and uncertainties—not raw emails.\n\nBefore approving, check Claude’s explanation of the intended file and destination. The profile should refer to the existing company description without changing it.\n\nIf the profile already exists, review the proposed edits before overwriting. Predict the intended output, unsupported assumptions and possible harmful changes.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Understand a file-writing request before approving it.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Use the approved analysis to create Communication_Profile.md. Save reusable guidance, short anonymised examples, evidence references and uncertainties—not raw emails.</p><p>Before approving, check Claude’s explanation of the intended file and destination. The profile should refer to the existing company description without changing it.</p><p>If the profile already exists, review the proposed edits before overwriting. Predict the intended output, unsupported assumptions and possible harmful changes.</p>"},"111":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nCreate a reusable writing skill from advice you have chosen and reviewed.\n\n### Notes\n\nSelect useful advice from the linked article or another source you trust. Ask skill-creator to turn it into a writing skill, then review the rules against your communication profile.\n\nRemove generic advice that weakens your voice. Test the skill under its chosen name, such as yourname-remove-ai-slop, in the environment where you intend to use it.\n\nYou are creating and testing your own reusable method here. Installation instructions are covered in Lab 1.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to creating and invoking Claude Code skills](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills)\n- [Five ways to preserve your brand voice in AI-assisted writing](https://www.writewide.com/5-steps-to-avoid-the-ai-clone-crisis-and-keep-your-brand-voice-alive/)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Create a reusable writing skill from advice you have chosen and reviewed.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Select useful advice from the linked article or another source you trust. Ask skill-creator to turn it into a writing skill, then review the rules against your communication profile.</p><p>Remove generic advice that weakens your voice. Test the skill under its chosen name, such as yourname-remove-ai-slop, in the environment where you intend to use it.</p><p>You are creating and testing your own reusable method here. Installation instructions are covered in Lab 1.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to creating and invoking Claude Code skills</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.writewide.com/5-steps-to-avoid-the-ai-clone-crisis-and-keep-your-brand-voice-alive/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Five ways to preserve your brand voice in AI-assisted writing</a></li></ul>"},"112":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nTest whether reply drafts are supported by your saved guidance.\n\n### Notes\n\nUse the company description, Communication_Profile.md and your writing skill to draft replies for two suitable examples.\n\nChallenge a sentence, greeting or sign-off that sounds wrong. Ask which saved rule supports it and what was assumed about the recipient or facts. Verify that the cited rule exists and applies.\n\nRequest a corrected version. Keep it in the conversation or explicitly approve saving a draft. Do not send a reply during the exercise.","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Test whether reply drafts are supported by your saved guidance.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Use the company description, Communication_Profile.md and your writing skill to draft replies for two suitable examples.</p><p>Challenge a sentence, greeting or sign-off that sounds wrong. Ask which saved rule supports it and what was assumed about the recipient or facts. Verify that the cited rule exists and applies.</p><p>Request a corrected version. Keep it in the conversation or explicitly approve saving a draft. Do not send a reply during the exercise.</p>"},"113":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nSet up a recurring email briefing using the guidance you have already approved.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**A cloud scheduled task** stores instructions and starts a new run at a chosen time. It can continue without your laptop, provided its required information and connected services are available in the cloud.\n\n### Notes\n\nUse Scheduled > New task and select Cloud where offered. Confirm the time zone; the example starts at 6:45 am on weekdays, not necessarily delivering at that exact minute.\n\nInclude the approved writing guidance in the saved instructions. Do not assume access to local files or earlier conversations.\n\nSummarise messages received since 5 pm on the previous working day; Monday includes the period since Friday. Limit each summary to 20 words and group by action needed, information and automated messages.\n\nEmail one briefing to yourself. Optional reply drafts and Slack notifications need separate access. Test with Run now and verify delivery; never send customer replies automatically.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to cloud scheduled tasks and their triggers](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Set up a recurring email briefing using the guidance you have already approved.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>A cloud scheduled task</strong> stores instructions and starts a new run at a chosen time. It can continue without your laptop, provided its required information and connected services are available in the cloud.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Use Scheduled &gt; New task and select Cloud where offered. Confirm the time zone; the example starts at 6:45 am on weekdays, not necessarily delivering at that exact minute.</p><p>Include the approved writing guidance in the saved instructions. Do not assume access to local files or earlier conversations.</p><p>Summarise messages received since 5 pm on the previous working day; Monday includes the period since Friday. Limit each summary to 20 words and group by action needed, information and automated messages.</p><p>Email one briefing to yourself. Optional reply drafts and Slack notifications need separate access. Test with Run now and verify delivery; never send customer replies automatically.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to cloud scheduled tasks and their triggers</a></li></ul>"},"114":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nConvert meeting minutes into organised records and approved tasks.\n\n### Concept Explanations\n\n**A task record** stores an action with details such as owner, priority, deadline and meeting source. Missing details should be marked unknown rather than guessed.\n\n### Notes\n\nCreate Minutes_To_Tasks.md to specify the minutes source, destination and task tool. Pasted minutes are acceptable.\n\nAfter a meeting, review the proposed storage location and actions, then approve saving and task creation. Mark missing owners or deadlines unknown and check for duplicates.\n\nWithout a task tool, the optional artefact board can use Task_Board.md in connected storage. Test that tasks survive reopening and remain accessible to later workflows; private artefact data is not automatically readable by a cloud task.\n\nTrigger this workflow after each meeting. Leave existing records unchanged unless an update is expressly requested.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to interactive artefacts and their limitations](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/17153992-what-are-artifacts-and-how-do-i-use-them)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Convert meeting minutes into organised records and approved tasks.</p><h3>Concept Explanations</h3><p><strong>A task record</strong> stores an action with details such as owner, priority, deadline and meeting source. Missing details should be marked unknown rather than guessed.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Create Minutes_To_Tasks.md to specify the minutes source, destination and task tool. Pasted minutes are acceptable.</p><p>After a meeting, review the proposed storage location and actions, then approve saving and task creation. Mark missing owners or deadlines unknown and check for duplicates.</p><p>Without a task tool, the optional artefact board can use Task_Board.md in connected storage. Test that tasks survive reopening and remain accessible to later workflows; private artefact data is not automatically readable by a cloud task.</p><p>Trigger this workflow after each meeting. Leave existing records unchanged unless an update is expressly requested.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/17153992-what-are-artifacts-and-how-do-i-use-them\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to interactive artefacts and their limitations</a></li></ul>"},"115":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nReceive a morning view of overdue, current and upcoming tasks.\n\n### Notes\n\nRead the existing task tool or shared Task_Board.md; do not create another board.\n\nRequest overdue tasks, tasks due today and those due in the next three days, with title, deadline and priority. Ask for three suggested priorities and their reasons.\n\nSave the rules in Morning_Briefing.md and include them in the cloud task. The weekday example starts at 6:45 am and emails only you without changing tasks.\n\nTest the task read and email delivery separately. Knowing your address or detecting an email tool does not prove a briefing was delivered.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to cloud scheduled tasks and their triggers](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Receive a morning view of overdue, current and upcoming tasks.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Read the existing task tool or shared Task_Board.md; do not create another board.</p><p>Request overdue tasks, tasks due today and those due in the next three days, with title, deadline and priority. Ask for three suggested priorities and their reasons.</p><p>Save the rules in Morning_Briefing.md and include them in the cloud task. The weekday example starts at 6:45 am and emails only you without changing tasks.</p><p>Test the task read and email delivery separately. Knowing your address or detecting an email tool does not prove a briefing was delivered.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to cloud scheduled tasks and their triggers</a></li></ul>"},"116":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nPrepare for upcoming meetings using information already in your own business records.\n\n### Notes\n\nChoose relevant meetings and whether to exclude recurring internal ones. Search your own email and minutes for each external attendee’s business connection, last interaction and unresolved actions—not the public web.\n\nLimit each person’s briefing to 50 words and mark guesses. Verify identity rather than relying on a matching name.\n\nSave Monday_Briefing.md and include its rules and accessible sources in the cloud task. The example runs Mondays at 7 am and emails only you. Test delivery and check the people identified. Do not change events or contact attendees.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to cloud scheduled tasks and their triggers](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Prepare for upcoming meetings using information already in your own business records.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Choose relevant meetings and whether to exclude recurring internal ones. Search your own email and minutes for each external attendee’s business connection, last interaction and unresolved actions—not the public web.</p><p>Limit each person’s briefing to 50 words and mark guesses. Verify identity rather than relying on a matching name.</p><p>Save Monday_Briefing.md and include its rules and accessible sources in the cloud task. The example runs Mondays at 7 am and emails only you. Test delivery and check the people identified. Do not change events or contact attendees.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to cloud scheduled tasks and their triggers</a></li></ul>"},"117":{"markdown":"### Slide Objective\n\nPrepare follow-up drafts for unanswered emails without sending them automatically.\n\n### Notes\n\nChoose the waiting period, search window and daily limit. The example checks 14 days of sent email, waits three working days and creates up to five drafts. Define working days and the time zone.\n\nInspect the conversation before deciding it is unanswered. Exclude bulk messages and skip existing follow-up drafts.\n\nSave Follow_Up_Chaser.md and include its rules and approved voice guidance in the cloud task. The weekday example starts at 8 am.\n\nTest actual draft creation. Review recipients, wording and promises before sending manually. The task may create new drafts, but must not send replies or alter existing messages. Optional notifications go only to you.\n\n### Sources\n\n- [Official guide to cloud scheduled tasks and their triggers](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines)","html":"<h3>Slide Objective</h3><p>Prepare follow-up drafts for unanswered emails without sending them automatically.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p>Choose the waiting period, search window and daily limit. The example checks 14 days of sent email, waits three working days and creates up to five drafts. Define working days and the time zone.</p><p>Inspect the conversation before deciding it is unanswered. Exclude bulk messages and skip existing follow-up drafts.</p><p>Save Follow_Up_Chaser.md and include its rules and approved voice guidance in the cloud task. The weekday example starts at 8 am.</p><p>Test actual draft creation. Review recipients, wording and promises before sending manually. The task may create new drafts, but must not send replies or alter existing messages. Optional notifications go only to you.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guide to cloud scheduled tasks and their triggers</a></li></ul>"}}};
const deck=window.DECKS?.[document.body.dataset.deck];
if(!deck)return;
for(const slide of deck.slides){const entry=notes[document.body.dataset.deck]?.[slide.n];if(entry){slide.note=entry.markdown;slide.participantNotesHTML=entry.html;}}
})();
